# Date strings that are not the form Catala writes

```catala
declaration scope Echo:
  input d content date
  output same content date

scope Echo:
  definition same equals d
```

A year has at least four digits:

```catala-test-cli
$ catala test-scope Echo --input {"d":"738-02-03"}
┌─[ERROR]─
│
│  Failed to validate JSON:
│    At /d, no case matched:
│      invalid date "738-02-03"
│      Unexpected string instead of object
│  
│  Expected JSON object of the form:
│    { "d": $date }
│    $date:
│      /* Catala date */
│      /^(?!-0000-)-?([0-9]{4}|[1-9][0-9]{4,})-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])$/
│      /* Accepts strings with the following format: YYYY-MM-DD, e.g.,
│         "1970-01-31", the year having more digits beyond 9999 and a minus
│         sign before 0 ("-0738-02-03") */
│      | { /* Accepts date objects: {"year":<int>, "month":<int>,
│             "day":<int>} */
│          "year": $integer,
│          "month": integer ∈ [1, 12],
│          "day": integer ∈ [1, 31] }
│    $integer:
│      /* Catala Integer */
│      integer | /^-?[0-9]+$/
│
└─
#return code 123#
```

and no leading zero beyond them, nor a sign before year 0:

```catala-test-cli
$ catala test-scope Echo --input {"d":"02000-01-01"}
┌─[ERROR]─
│
│  Failed to validate JSON:
│    At /d, no case matched:
│      invalid date "02000-01-01"
│      Unexpected string instead of object
│  
│  Expected JSON object of the form:
│    { "d": $date }
│    $date:
│      /* Catala date */
│      /^(?!-0000-)-?([0-9]{4}|[1-9][0-9]{4,})-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])$/
│      /* Accepts strings with the following format: YYYY-MM-DD, e.g.,
│         "1970-01-31", the year having more digits beyond 9999 and a minus
│         sign before 0 ("-0738-02-03") */
│      | { /* Accepts date objects: {"year":<int>, "month":<int>,
│             "day":<int>} */
│          "year": $integer,
│          "month": integer ∈ [1, 12],
│          "day": integer ∈ [1, 31] }
│    $integer:
│      /* Catala Integer */
│      integer | /^-?[0-9]+$/
│
└─
#return code 123#
```

```catala-test-cli
$ catala test-scope Echo --input {"d":"-0000-01-01"}
┌─[ERROR]─
│
│  Failed to validate JSON:
│    At /d, no case matched:
│      invalid date "-0000-01-01"
│      Unexpected string instead of object
│  
│  Expected JSON object of the form:
│    { "d": $date }
│    $date:
│      /* Catala date */
│      /^(?!-0000-)-?([0-9]{4}|[1-9][0-9]{4,})-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])$/
│      /* Accepts strings with the following format: YYYY-MM-DD, e.g.,
│         "1970-01-31", the year having more digits beyond 9999 and a minus
│         sign before 0 ("-0738-02-03") */
│      | { /* Accepts date objects: {"year":<int>, "month":<int>,
│             "day":<int>} */
│          "year": $integer,
│          "month": integer ∈ [1, 12],
│          "day": integer ∈ [1, 31] }
│    $integer:
│      /* Catala Integer */
│      integer | /^-?[0-9]+$/
│
└─
#return code 123#
```

A date of the calendar:

```catala-test-cli
$ catala test-scope Echo --input {"d":"2023-02-29"}
┌─[ERROR]─
│
│  Failed to validate JSON:
│    At /d, no case matched:
│      invalid date "2023-02-29"
│      Unexpected string instead of object
│  
│  Expected JSON object of the form:
│    { "d": $date }
│    $date:
│      /* Catala date */
│      /^(?!-0000-)-?([0-9]{4}|[1-9][0-9]{4,})-(0[1-9]|1[0-2])-(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])$/
│      /* Accepts strings with the following format: YYYY-MM-DD, e.g.,
│         "1970-01-31", the year having more digits beyond 9999 and a minus
│         sign before 0 ("-0738-02-03") */
│      | { /* Accepts date objects: {"year":<int>, "month":<int>,
│             "day":<int>} */
│          "year": $integer,
│          "month": integer ∈ [1, 12],
│          "day": integer ∈ [1, 31] }
│    $integer:
│      /* Catala Integer */
│      integer | /^-?[0-9]+$/
│
└─
#return code 123#
```

A year beyond machine integers is an integer too large for the computation:

```catala-test-cli
$ catala test-scope Echo --input {"d":"99999999999999999999-01-01"}
┌─[ERROR]─
│
│  During evaluation: an integer is too large for this computation.
│  Reading the JSON value at /d.
│
└─
#return code 123#
```
